term:Best-Efforts Impact Assessment (Kor.deep_dive:
Korea's impact assessment is **best efforts only**, and that softness is the fact the exam tests. **Art. 35** says operators providing high-impact AI **"shall make efforts"** to assess its impact on fundamental rights **in advance**. It is not a mandatory assessment: skipping it carries **no fine**, and it is **not a trigger for fact-finding** under Art. 40 either.

The incentive is procurement, not penalty. **Art. 35(2)** requires national institutions (the state, local governments and public institutions) that use high-impact AI to **give priority to products or services that have been assessed**. So the assessment is voluntary but commercially useful for anyone selling to the Korean public sector.

The **contents** (Decree Art. 28(1)):

- the individuals or groups affected;
- the fundamental rights at stake;
- social and economic impacts;
- usage patterns;
- evaluation indicators and calculation methods;
- measures to prevent and mitigate risks and to recover losses; and
- an improvement plan where one is needed.

Who performs it: **the operator itself, or a third party it commissions** (Decree Art. 28(2)). **Since 21 July 2026** the assessment must also reflect the characteristics of **AI-vulnerable groups** in light of the product or service (Act No. 21311); Decree Art. 1-2 defines those groups, including people with disabilities and people aged 65 and over, and Decree Art. 28(1)1 was amended so that identifying affected groups takes them into account.

The EU contrast is exact. The EU's **fundamental rights impact assessment (Art. 27)** is **mandatory**, but only for certain deployers: bodies governed by public law, private entities providing public services, and deployers of credit-scoring or life and health insurance pricing systems. Korea's is available to all high-impact operators, mandatory for none, and backed by a procurement carrot rather than a fine.

term:AI Importer.deep_dive:
Article 3(6) defines an importer as an entity established in the Union that places on the market an AI system bearing the name or trademark of a provider established outside the Union. The importer is the Union's first point of contact with a foreign provider's system, and Article 23 gives it verification duties rather than design duties.

Before placing a high-risk system on the market, the importer must confirm that:

- the provider has carried out the relevant conformity assessment procedure;
- the provider has drawn up the technical documentation;
- the system bears the CE marking and is accompanied by the EU Declaration of Conformity and instructions for use;
- the provider has appointed an authorised representative in the Union (Article 22).

If the importer has reason to believe the system is not compliant, it must not place it on the market. It must also put its own name and contact address on the system or its packaging, keep the documentation for **10 years**, and cooperate with competent authorities.

Distinguish the importer from the **authorised representative**: the representative is mandated by the provider to act on its behalf, while the importer independently places the goods on the market. Both are separate from the **distributor**, which passes an already-placed system further down the chain.

term:Prohibited AI.deep_dive:
Article 5 of the EU AI Act bans a closed list of practices outright. As enacted (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) the list has **eight** points, applying since 2 February 2025:

- subliminal or purposefully manipulative techniques that materially distort behaviour and cause significant harm;
- exploitation of vulnerabilities from age, disability or social or economic situation;
- social scoring leading to detrimental or disproportionate treatment;
- individual criminal-offence risk prediction based solely on profiling or personality traits;
- untargeted scraping of facial images to build recognition databases;
- emotion recognition in workplaces and educational institutions;
- biometric categorisation by sensitive traits;
- real-time remote biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement, with three narrow exceptions requiring prior judicial authorisation.

As amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744), two more points, (ba) and (bb), apply from **2 December 2026**.

Prohibited practices sit at the top of the four-tier pyramid and carry the heaviest penalty: up to **EUR 35 million or 7%** of worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher (Article 99(3)). The distinction the exam draws is between a practice that is **banned entirely** and a high-risk system that is permitted subject to obligations.

term:Design Defect.deep_dive:
A design defect claim says the product was built as intended but the design itself is unreasonably dangerous. The usual test is **risk-utility**: weigh the risk of harm against the product's utility and ask whether a **reasonable alternative design** could have reduced the risk without destroying its usefulness.

Applied to AI, plaintiffs tend to allege one of a small set of failures:

- training data that was insufficient or unrepresentative;
- a safer model architecture that was available and ignored;
- design that omitted human oversight where it was needed;
- a safer default behaviour that was feasible.

Pinning a defect in an adaptive system is hard. Behaviour is context-sensitive, models drift after deployment, and outputs are probabilistic, so a claimant may struggle to show that the design was defective rather than the data, the deployment or the user. The EU's revised Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853 answers part of this: the defect test expressly weighs **the effect of any ability to continue to learn or acquire new features after the product is placed on the market** (Article 7(2)(c)). It also presumes defect where the defendant fails to disclose evidence, the product breaches mandatory safety requirements, or there is an obvious malfunction, and presumes causation where the damage is typical of the defect (Article 10). Machine learning is named as a case of technical complexity that can justify the presumption.

Design defect is distinct from a manufacturing defect (a departure from the design) and from failure to warn (inadequate instructions).
